# Mutation cases for §scxml-3.12.2 — an `error.*` event the processor raised
# that no transition answered, and the host that is driving the machine can
# find out.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/unhandled_error_is_observable.cases`.
#
# The clause requires the processor to signal its own failures as `error.*` on
# the INTERNAL queue and says in the same breath that they "are ignored if no
# transition is found that matches them". Being ignored is the clause; being
# unable to say it happened is not — and the party that cannot see it is the
# host, which never wrote the document and cannot read the failure anywhere in
# the configuration.
#
# The trap these cases exist for is the third one below. The sibling axis
# (`discarded_event_is_observable`) drew its boundary at the external queue on
# the stated ground that an unmatched internal event has both ends inside the
# document. That is right for an author's `<raise>` and wrong for an error
# event, whose sender is the engine — so a count that drops the name test
# counts the author's own `<raise event="unheard"/>` as an engine failure, and
# one that keeps only the name test but not the outcome counts a HANDLED error.
# The fixture routes one event name (`boom`) to both outcomes by state so
# neither can be faked.
#
# The C++ engine and the C11 template halves of the same axis live in
# `unhandled_error_is_observable_ctest.cases`.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-rust-tests --test unhandled_error_is_observable
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs
mutation_targets backends/rust/runtime/src/helpers/event_matching.rs
mutation_oracles backends/rust/tests/tests/unhandled_error_is_observable.rs

# ── The count itself ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The engine goes back to knowing and not saying. Every driver's first
# assertion is this one.
#
# The increment is what this removes, rather than the whole `if`. A mutation
# that deletes the condition leaves `outcome` unread, and `warnings = deny`
# turns that into a tree that does not compile — which the harness can only
# report as INCONCLUSIVE, so the case would witness nothing. Measured here:
# both of the first two drafts died that way.

mutation_case "the internal drain drops the outcome again" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """                self.unhandled_error_events = self.unhandled_error_events.saturating_add(1);""",
     """                self.unhandled_error_events = self.unhandled_error_events.saturating_add(0);""")
PY

# ── Counting an error the document DID answer ──────────────────────
#
# Widening the outcome test to accept both arms leaves the name test, which is
# true for the `guarded` run as well — so the document's own error handling is
# reported as a silent failure. `an_error_the_document_handled_is_not_counted`
# is the assertion that separates the two, and it is the reason `boom` appears
# in two states.

mutation_case "every error is counted, answered or not" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            if outcome == EventOutcome::Discarded && is_error {""",
     """            if matches!(
                outcome,
                EventOutcome::Discarded | EventOutcome::Taken { .. }
            ) && is_error
            {""")
PY

# ── Counting the author's own unmatched raise ──────────────────────
#
# The boundary this axis is really about. Dropping the name test counts every
# unmatched internal event, which is exactly the thing the sibling axis
# deliberately does not count — `whisper` raises `unheard`, nothing answers it,
# and both ends of that are in the document.

mutation_case "any unmatched internal event counts as an engine error" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            if outcome == EventOutcome::Discarded && is_error {""",
     """            if outcome == EventOutcome::Discarded {""")
PY

# ── The classifier ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# §scxml-3.12.2 reserves the whole `error.` prefix. A classifier that answers
# for nothing silences the axis; one that answers for everything turns it into
# the case above, one layer deeper than the call site.

mutation_case "no name is an error name" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/helpers/event_matching.rs",
     """    event_name.starts_with("error.")
}""",
     """    event_name.starts_with("no.name.starts.with.this.")
}""")
PY

mutation_case "every name is an error name" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/helpers/event_matching.rs",
     """    event_name.starts_with("error.")
}""",
     """    event_name.starts_with("")
}""")
PY

# The prefix is a prefix, not a substring: an event the author named
# `retry.error.execution` is the document's, not the engine's.

mutation_case "the error prefix is matched anywhere in the name" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/helpers/event_matching.rs",
     """    event_name.starts_with("error.")
}""",
     """    event_name.contains("error.")
}""")
PY

# ── Naming the error ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The count says something failed; a host repairing it needs the class —
# `error.execution` and `error.communication` are two different repairs.
# Leaving it at `None` still counts correctly.

mutation_case "the unhandled error is counted but never named" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """                self.last_unhandled_error = Some(event_with_meta.event);""",
     """                self.last_unhandled_error = None;""")
PY

# ── What the error SAYS ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# §scxml-3.12.2 closes with "platforms MAY include additional information
# about the nature of the error in the 'data' field". A MAY is precisely why
# this drifted rather than failing: the C++ Interpreter carried the failing
# construct at every site it had, the C++ AOT backend at 8 sites of 37, and
# the other five backends at 0 of 168 — three answers to one question inside
# one repository, and nothing that could notice.
#
# `detail` in the fixture is what a document that ANSWERS the error reads,
# which is the party the clause is written for.

# ⚠ NO CASE FOR THE MESSAGE ITSELF YET, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE FINDING.
# `platform_error` demonstrably puts the message in `_event.data` — the unit
# test in `event.rs` holds that — and the DOCUMENT still receives nil on an
# internal-queue error, while `_event.name` on the same event arrives fine.
# The break is between the queue and the script binding, it predates this
# round, and no fixture covers it (`event_data_arrives_as_sent` drives with
# `raise_external`, so only the external queue is measured). Registry E6.
# Until that lands, a case here would report INCONCLUSIVE for a reason that
# has nothing to do with the contract it claims to guard.

# ⚠ NO CASE FOR `event_type` YET, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE FINDING. The same
# constructor also fixes the metadata's classification — `new()` defaults to
# External and every Rust raise site passed that, the one backend of six that
# labelled its OWN errors as the document's. It cannot be defended here:
# generated code recomputes the script-visible `_event.type` from the event
# NAME and overwrites whatever the metadata said, so flipping the field back
# to External changes nothing any test can read. One fact, two producers, the
# later one always winning. Closing that is its own round (registry E4); a
# case added before then would SURVIVE for a reason that has nothing to do
# with the contract it claims to guard.

# ── The selection this drain exists to run ─────────────────────────
#
# The count is a byproduct of the drain, not its job. Folding the selection
# into the condition — which is what the first draft of this code did — skips
# `execute_transition` for every event that is not an error, so nothing in the
# document runs. The fixture's `<assign>` counters are what notice.

mutation_case "the selection becomes the right-hand side of the error test" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/runtime/src/engine.rs",
     """            let outcome = self.execute_transition(event_with_meta.event);
            self.handling_error_event = false;""",
     """            let outcome = if is_error {
                self.execute_transition(event_with_meta.event)
            } else {
                EventOutcome::Taken {
                    configuration_changed: false,
                }
            };
            self.handling_error_event = false;""")
PY
